Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers on industrial field devices
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP's read_holding_registers returns a plain list or None instead of a response object with isError()/.registers, requiring 
    │   rewritten error-checking logic; modbus-tk uses a master.execute() call pattern with modbus_tcp.TcpMaster and different function-code constants instead 
    │   of named methods like read_holding_registers; unit/slave id parameter naming and defaults differ across libraries (unit= vs slave_id= vs positional); 
    │   connection lifecycle differs: pymodbus uses .connect(), pyModbusTCP uses .open(), modbus-tk uses implicit connection on master creation
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP master/client functionality capable of connecting to a host:port and reading/writing holding registers, which is
        the exact operation performed here (temperature/pressure reads, valve writes). They are functionally interchangeable at the protocol level, though each
        has a distinct API surface (response objects vs raw lists, error handling model, unit/slave id parameter naming).

